<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tsu, Mie</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tsu is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, on the western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a>. Its medieval port was ruined by the earthquake of 1498, and it was refounded as a castle town by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Tōdō Takatora</a> after he received the domain in 1608. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> Tsu prospered from pilgrims travelling on the road to the Ise shrines, and it later became the capital of Mie Prefecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tsu-mie.jpg" alt="Tsu, Mie" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 水だらけのプール · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tsu_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
